CAT = "ukaplhActivities"; SEG = "ACT"
Q = [
("Which of these is a licensable activity under the Licensing Act 2003?",
 "Late night refreshment", ["Selling soft drinks", "Selling cold sandwiches", "Showing live TV sport"],
 "The licensable activities are the sale of alcohol, the supply of alcohol to club members, regulated entertainment and late night refreshment. Live television broadcasts are not regulated entertainment."),
("Between which hours does selling hot food to the public count as late night refreshment?",
 "11 pm and 5 am", ["Midnight and 6 am", "10 pm and 6 am", "11 pm and 7 am"],
 "Late night refreshment is the supply of hot food or hot drink to the public between 11 pm and 5 am, on or off the premises."),
("What does late night refreshment cover?",
 "Hot food or hot drink", ["Any food or any drink", "Alcohol served with a meal", "Cold food sold to take away"],
 "Only hot food or hot drink counts, such as a takeaway selling kebabs after 11 pm. Cold food and soft drinks are not licensable."),
("Which of these is regulated entertainment?",
 "A performance of a play", ["A televised football match", "Background music in a shop", "A pub quiz night"],
 "Regulated entertainment includes plays, films, indoor sport, boxing or wrestling, live and recorded music and dance, subject to exemptions. Live TV, incidental background music and quizzes are not."),
("What authorises a members' club to supply alcohol to its members?",
 "A club premises certificate", ["A standard premises licence", "The steward's personal licence", "A temporary event notice"],
 "A qualifying club supplies alcohol to members under a club premises certificate. No personal licence holder or DPS is needed, because members are not buying from a retailer."),
("What is the minimum number of members for a qualifying club supplying alcohol?",
 "25", ["10", "50", "100"],
 "A qualifying club must have at least 25 members, be established in good faith and meet the other conditions in the Act."),
("In a qualifying club, how long must pass between applying for membership and being admitted?",
 "At least two days", ["At least one day", "At least seven days", "At least 28 days"],
 "There must be an interval of at least two days between a person's nomination or application and their admission to membership, so a club cannot sign people up at the door."),
("What is the maximum number of people allowed at an event under a temporary event notice?",
 "499", ["500", "300", "999"],
 "No more than 499 people, including staff and performers, may be on the premises at any one time. Larger events need a premises licence."),
("What is the longest a single event under a temporary event notice may last?",
 "168 hours", ["24 hours", "72 hours", "96 hours"],
 "A TEN can cover an event of up to 168 hours, which is seven days."),
("How many temporary event notices can be given for the same premises in one calendar year?",
 "15", ["12", "20", "50"],
 "The limit is 15 TENs per premises per year. It was raised to 20 for 2022 and 2023 only, then returned to 15."),
("What is the most days a premises can be covered by temporary event notices in one calendar year?",
 "21", ["15", "26", "28"],
 "TENs at one premises may cover no more than 21 days in total per calendar year. The 26-day limit applied only in 2022 and 2023."),
("How many temporary event notices can a personal licence holder give in a year?",
 "50", ["5", "15", "100"],
 "A personal licence holder may give up to 50 TENs a year, of which no more than 10 may be late TENs. Someone without a personal licence may give 5."),
("How many temporary event notices can someone without a personal licence give in a year?",
 "5", ["2", "10", "12"],
 "A premises user without a personal licence can give up to 5 TENs a year, of which no more than 2 may be late TENs."),
("How much notice is needed for a standard temporary event notice?",
 "At least 10 working days", ["At least 5 working days", "At least 28 calendar days", "At least 2 working days"],
 "A standard TEN must be received at least 10 clear working days before the event. Notices with less time are late TENs, if they still allow 5 working days."),
("When is a temporary event notice treated as a late TEN?",
 "5 to 9 working days before", ["1 to 4 working days before", "10 to 14 working days before", "On the day of the event"],
 "A late TEN is given between 5 and 9 working days before the event. A notice with less than 5 working days is not valid at all."),
("Besides the licensing authority, who must receive a temporary event notice?",
 "Police and environmental health", ["Fire service and trading standards", "Planning and building control", "Neighbours within 100 metres"],
 "The premises user sends the TEN to the licensing authority, the police and the local authority environmental health team. Only the police and environmental health can object."),
("How long do the police and environmental health have to object to a temporary event notice?",
 "3 working days", ["7 days", "10 working days", "28 days"],
 "They have three working days from receiving the notice to object on the basis of the licensing objectives."),
("The police object to a late temporary event notice. What happens?",
 "The event cannot go ahead", ["A hearing must be held", "It goes ahead with conditions", "The organiser can appeal at once"],
 "There is no hearing for a late TEN. If the police or environmental health object, the authority issues a counter notice and the event cannot take place."),
("What minimum gap must there be between events by the same premises user at the same premises?",
 "24 hours", ["12 hours", "48 hours", "7 days"],
 "There must be at least 24 hours between the end of one temporary event and the start of the next by the same person or an associate at the same premises."),
("What is the minimum age to give a temporary event notice?",
 "18", ["16", "21", "25"],
 "The premises user giving a TEN must be an individual aged 18 or over. Companies cannot give TENs."),
("Selling alcohol at premises with no licence, certificate or TEN is which offence?",
 "Unauthorised licensable activity", ["Persistent selling to children", "Allowing disorderly conduct", "Obstructing an authorised person"],
 "Carrying on or attempting to carry on a licensable activity without an authorisation, or not in line with its conditions, is the offence of unauthorised licensable activity under section 136."),
("Which defence is available to a charge of unauthorised licensable activity?",
 "Due diligence", ["Ignorance of the law", "The customer insisted", "It was a first offence"],
 "A person charged can show the act was due to a mistake, information from someone else, another person's act or a cause beyond their control, and that they took all reasonable precautions and exercised due diligence."),
("Storing alcohol at unlicensed premises intending to sell it there is:",
 "An offence under the Act", ["Legal until a sale is made", "Legal if under 10 litres", "Legal if it is locked away"],
 "Keeping alcohol on premises for unauthorised sale or supply is a separate offence, as is exposing it for unauthorised sale."),
("On premises licensed for on-sales, recorded music between 8 am and 11 pm is not licensable for audiences up to how many?",
 "500", ["100", "250", "1,000"],
 "Deregulation means recorded music on alcohol on-licensed premises between 8 am and 11 pm is not licensable for audiences of up to 500. Licensing authorities can restore conditions after a review."),
("What licence does unamplified live music need between 8 am and 11 pm?",
 "None, at any audience size", ["A premises licence always", "A TEN for each performance", "A licence over 100 people"],
 "Unamplified live music between 8 am and 11 pm is not regulated entertainment at any venue, whatever the audience size."),
("Indoor sport between 8 am and 11 pm is not licensable for audiences up to how many?",
 "1,000", ["200", "500", "2,000"],
 "Indoor sporting events between 8 am and 11 pm are exempt for audiences of up to 1,000 people. Boxing and wrestling are treated differently."),
("A premises licence has no effect for alcohol at premises used mainly as which of these?",
 "A garage selling fuel", ["A community village hall", "A members' sports club", "A hotel with a bar"],
 "Premises used primarily as a garage, meaning for selling petrol or derv or for repairing vehicles, and motorway service areas are excluded premises for alcohol sales."),
("Selling alcohol from a road vehicle, such as a van, while it is moving is:",
 "Banned in all cases", ["Allowed under a TEN", "Allowed with a personal licence", "Allowed before 11 pm"],
 "Section 156 makes it an offence to sell alcohol on or from a vehicle while it is not permanently or temporarily parked, whatever authorisation is held. Trains, aircraft and boats on a journey are covered by separate rules."),
]
